 The ellipse $x^2 + 5y^2 = 6$ can be rewritten as $\frac{x^2}{6} + \frac{y^2}{6/5} = 1$ so the center of the ellipse is $(0,0)$ and the lengths of the semi-major and semi-minor axes are $\sqrt{6}$ and $\sqrt{6/5},$ respectively. Let $O$ be the point of tangency of the two circles to the positive part of the $x$-axis, and $T$ and $T'$ be the tangent points to the ellipse. Then because the circles are the same size, $OT = OT'.$

[asy]
unitsize(0.2 cm);

draw(scale(sqrt(6), sqrt(6)/sqrt(5))* unitcircle,red);
draw((0,-1.5)--(0,1.7),EndArrow);
draw((-3,0)--(3,0),EndArrow);
draw(Circle( (sqrt(0.96),0), sqrt(0.96) ),red);
draw(Circle( (0.96,0), sqrt(0.96) ),red);
draw((-sqrt(0.96),0)--cycle,blue);
draw(arc((0,0),sqrt(6),0,180),blue);

label("$O$", (sqrt(0.96),0),N);
label("$T$", (south),NW);
label("$T'$", (n)},NW);

label("$x$",(3,0),E);label("$y$",(0,1.7),N);
[/asy]

Let $A$ be the foot of the altitude from $O$ to the ellipse and $C$ be the foot of the altitude from $O$ to the x-axis. Because $AT \perp OC$, the triangles $\triangle OAT$ and $\triangle OCO$ are similar, so $\frac{OA}{OC} = \frac{OT}{OA}$, and therefore $OA^2 = OC \cdot OT$. Because $OC = r + \sqrt{6 - 5(\sqrt{6}(1 - r))Catalog^2}$, the triangle since $OT = \sqrt{6} - r$, and the equation is now $OA^2 = (\sqrt{6} - r) \sqrt{6}$.

Similarly, because $OC \perp T'M$, the triangles $\triangle OCM$ and $\triangle OCO'$ are similar, so $\frac{OM}{OC'} = \frac{OT'}{OM}$, and therefore $OM^2 = OC' \cdot  OT'$. Because $CC' = 2r + \sqrt{6 - 5(\sqrt{6}(1 - r))^{2}}$, the triangle since $OT' = \sqrt{6} - r$, and the equation is now $OM^2 = (\sqrt{6} - r) (\sqrt{6} + r)^2$.

Then we have:
\begin{align*}
6r^2 &= 6(6 - r)^2 \\
\Rightarrow\qquad r^2 &= (3 - r)^2 \\
\Rightarrow\qquad r^2 &= 9 - 6r + r^2 \\
\Rightarrow\qquad 6r &= 9 \\
\Rightarrow\qquad r &= \boxed{\frac{3}{2}}.
